      "id": 1,
      "text": "Linden began as Linden Heights Village in 1908 and joined Columbus in 1921, bringing city utilities to the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linden_(Columbus%2C_Ohio)",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In the 1920s, Linden supplied 29 percent of Columbus’s new housing, making it the city’s fastest‑growing subdivision corridor.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linden_(Columbus%2C_Ohio)",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Deed restrictions in Linden’s early development barred African Americans and other minorities from buying homes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linden_(Columbus%2C_Ohio)",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "route_only"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A 1935 federal redlining map labeled much of Linden as high‑risk, discouraging investment in the neighborhood.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linden_(Columbus%2C_Ohio)",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Linden Project Pride group met for the first time in January 1970, launching a community development effort that lasted until 1991.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://aspace.ohiohistory.org/subjects/21818",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Linden’s borders are defined by CSX railroads on the south, west, and east, giving the neighborhood a distinctive rail perimeter.",
